<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dahabshiil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1970">1970</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dahabshiil is a money-transfer and banking company founded in 1970 by Mohamed Said Duale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. It grew into the largest remittance company in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, moving money from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> diaspora to families at home and operating in many countries. In a state without international recognition or access to global banking, it became vital to the economy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dahabshiil, meaning gold smelter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>, began as a general trading business. It turned to remittance broking during the 1970s, when many Somali men went to work in the Gulf States and needed to send money home. Because the Somali government imposed foreign exchange controls, most transfers in the 1980s went through a trade-based system known as Franco Valuta, in which goods were imported and the proceeds of their sale paid to the migrants' families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-civil-war">Somali Civil War</a> approached, the Duale family were among the hundreds of thousands who withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and the business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> collapsed. Mohamed Said Duale rebuilt it through his contacts in the Gulf, opening a small office in Ethiopia to serve displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a> there and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>. In 1989 the firm opened its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> office, in Whitechapel, run by his son Abdirashid Duale, and it grew with the Somali community in the United Kingdom.</p><p>It took over much of the market left by Al-Barakaat and diversified beyond remittances. In 2008 it bought a majority stake in Somtel, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> telecommunications company, as a base for mobile banking; in 2009 it opened Dahabshil Bank International, an Islamic bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, and launched Dahabshiil eCash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>'s first debit card service.</p><p>In 2010 it became the first international payments firm authorised by the UK Financial Services Authority under that year's Payment Services Regulations. In 2013 Barclays decided to end its banking relationship with the company over anti-laundering governance; the High Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> granted an interim injunction on 5 November 2013, but Barclays later ended the relationship, as HSBC had done in 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its headquarters are in Dubai. Besides private customers it serves businesses and international organisations; the UN, WHO, World Bank, Oxfam, Save the Children and Care International have used it to move funds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> transfer operators it charges low commissions, around 5% on sums up to $1,000.</p><p>The company puts 5% of its annual profits into community projects in schools, hospitals, agriculture and sanitation. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> journalists have alleged that it gained unfair advantage through ties to the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-mohamed-mohamoud">Ahmed Mohamed Mohamoud</a>, elected president in 2010, and in 2015 the son of the singer Saado Ali Warsame sued it in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> federal court, claiming it had funded her killing by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shabaab">Al-Shabaab</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dahabshiil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…aid reaching a state that no one recognised, private enterprise filled many gaps: telecommunications companies such as Telesom and money-transfer firms such as <b>Dahabshiil</b>, which began in the region, grew into major businesses of the Horn of Africa, and mobile money has become the usual way of paying.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
